BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:z297vWWgOrfsLta59kzOY
SUMMARY:Defective Futures: Disability and Eugenics in American and British 
	Literature (1896 – 1936)
DTSTAMP:20260805T111656Z
DTSTART:20260819T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Stacie Cruz  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defen
	se  Department: English  Location: Herring Hall 255  "Defective Futures: D
	isability and Eugenics in American and British Literature (1896 – 1936)" i
	nterrogates the ways that novels in the late 19th to early 20th centuries 
	utilize eugenic logic and discourse\, arguing that the widely popularized 
	concept of "defectiveness" allows us to think about the interconnecting lo
	gic behind the marginalization of particular bodyminds. To this end\, I fo
	cus on the ways in which novels of the period apply and also (often at the
	 same time) critique eugenic discourse. Utilizing the widespread use of th
	e concept of defectiveness as a term that predates our current understandi
	ngs of disability\, I interrogate the shifting borders between who is or i
	s not defective depending on who is wielding the term. Analyzing works by 
	authors such as Virginia Woolf\, Charlotte Perkins Gilman\, and H.G. Wells
	\, I analyze how novels represent their relationship to the future and how
	 they narratively imagined and created futures where various populations d
	o or do not exist\, regardless of whether the narrative itself is critical
	 of the project of eugenics as a whole.  &#160\;  Each chapter critiques s
	upposed dichotomies such as perfection and deformity\, humanness and anima
	lity\, and madness and rationality. And it interrogates widely held belief
	s\, past and present\, about what a body is\, and what it should do\, and 
	what a mind is\, and should do. This project centers rather than marginali
	zes the limit cases that most scholarly discussions of the future ignore: 
	those whose bodies and minds require us to examine our discomfort about al
	lowing pain\, defectiveness\, and bodymind suffering into the future. I su
	ggest that the eugenic logic that inflects the novels I analyze likewise i
	nfluences how we think about the future today. While many of us understand
	ably desire the creation of more just futures\, I nonetheless caution that
	 understanding the future as a utopian place where things will be "better"
	 than they currently are often relies on eugenic thinking that eliminates 
	"defective" populations altogether.  Defense Zoom Meeting: https://riceuni
	versity.zoom.us/j/94213221284?pwd=O6W0zhOPlxf4dEZiOOcoNdGdHRyKtd.1  (Meeti
	ng ID: 942 1322 1284 / Passcode: 1)  One tap mobile: +13462487799\,\,94213
	221284# US (Houston)  Join instructions:  https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/m
	eetings/94213221284/invitations?signature=6y2-3NwMfNUMEY1Ehp6Ha2A28K9fiFiQ
	TIQEOtEvOu4  (Department : English)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/live/events
	/436393-defective-futures-disability-and-eugenics-in\n\nFrom Rice Universi
	ty - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/436393-defective-futures-disability
	-and-eugenics-in
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Herring Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
